WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.280
Minor external bleeding typically comes from small scrapes, shallow cuts, or abrasions that only affect the top layers of your skin.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:14.360
Because this type of bleeding is slow, your goal is to get it under control quickly by using that keyword we just talked about.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:16.040
Pressure.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:20.719
First step is to apply firm pressure to the wound. If the person is alert,

00:00:20.719 --> 00:00:27.020
you can actually ask them to hold the pressure themselves while you take a moment to get your supplies and put on your gloves.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:28.940
Once the bleeding slows down,

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:35.180
you'll want to wash the area thoroughly with soap and water. If it is permitted in your setting and the bleeding has stopped,

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:41.560
you can apply a bit of antibiotic ointment and then cover the wound with a clean dressing or a simple adhesive bandage.

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:47.380
Advise the person to follow up with a health care provider if the wound is gaping and might need stitches or

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:53.980
if they were injured by a dirty object, which could mean they need a tetanus shot. Now, let's put those materials

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:58.619
we passed out to use. I want everyone to start by putting on your gloves right now.

00:00:58.779 --> 00:01:05.180
Once you have them on, take your first gauze square and place it firmly over the injury to apply initial pressure.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:12.900
Once in place, never pull off that initial layer of gauze as it can dislodge the clots that are starting to form and interrupt the pressure.

